  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## THE GREAT RETROFIT v3 — NetLogo port

**Every number in this model is a placeholder.** The defaults make the structure run and keep the tests meaningful; nothing computed from them is a finding (which households benefit, how much the state spends, how the delivery modes rank). Do not quote any output of this model as a result.

This is a single-file NetLogo 7 port of `v3/src/retrofit_v3/` (the Python reference; `packs/great-retrofit/runtime.js` mirrors it). It asks: *given who can pay, who gets the subsidy, and how the work is delivered, which households retrofit, and what does the state spend?* Households retrofit when the NPV over the time they expect to stay is positive and when they can finance the upfront cost. There is no ideology or solidarity premium; regressivity is meant to *emerge* from credit access plus tax capacity.

### HOW TO USE

1. Press **setup**. The synthetic scene is built (placeholder rules: lognormal footprints, random tenure, unit sizes split deterministically) and one building is placed per patch, row-major from the top-left.
2. Press **go** (monthly step) or **go to end** (runs all remaining months).
3. Slides, choosers and switches on the left/centre are the policy levers and household parameters. Everything resets on **setup**.

Map legend: red = no unit done, orange = partly done, green = fully retrofitted, gray = rental flat (not modelled, never adopts, never votes). Condos with two or more units carry a shared-parts vote (floor-area weighted, default threshold 2/3).

### WHAT IS MODELLED

- Owner-occupied homes, condos (unit decision + shared parts by vote), rental stub (never retrofits).
- Three delivery modes: `rebate_market` (market vendor, price + vendor margin, rebate after a lag, capped by remaining tax capacity unless refundable), `passthrough` (state pays the whole price at adoption, vendor margin applies), `public_service` (state pays, public overhead instead of margin, capacity-limited: `public-capacity` share of all units per year, fractional slots carried; when more candidates than slots, a fixed per-household draw orders the queue; a shared measure uses ceil(units x shared_share) slots).
- Means test: when on, only households in the bottom `means-test-max-quintile` quintiles are in the scheme (rebate, passthrough or public service); everyone else buys on the market with no subsidy.
- Household finances: savings first (scaled by `savings-usable-share`), remainder by a level-annuity loan over `loan-term-months` at `loan-rate` (+ `loan-rate-risk-premium` below the credit income threshold). Credit: `income_tested` (threshold + loan-to-income cap), `open`, or `none`.
- NPV with perfect foresight: PV of energy savings over the remaining stay (growth `rising` by default) + PV of rebate − cash from savings − PV of loan payments. No resale value, no altruism, no partial measures.
- Condo canvassing: advocates (supporters or payers) spend min(free hours, `canvass-target-hours`) per week, limited by a weekly time budget (long hours at both ends of the income scale — a placeholder hypothesis). Pressure raises a neighbour's monthly consideration chance (neighbour effect + canvass effect, both capped at 1).
- Shared-measure voting: support is permanent and is granted when a household's own share of the shared measure is financeable and NPV-positive. The measure passes in the first month the floor-area share of supporters reaches `vote-threshold`, and only if **every** owner can finance their share; once passed, all owners pay, supporter or not.
- Tax capacity: a non-refundable rebate is capped by the household's remaining tax capacity (`tax-left`), which each rebate reduces. One year of capacity serves all of a household's payments; no carry-forward.

### DETERMINISM

All randomness flows through a mulberry32 PRNG ported from `v3/common/rng.py`, including a stateless `hash_uniform(seed, agent, month)` for per-household, per-month draws (changing a policy lever never shifts another household's draws). The same seed reproduces the same history. Agent iteration is in unit-id order throughout, as in the Python model.

Known differences from the Python/JS ports (ulp-level, may accumulate): `round()` here rounds half away from zero, Python rounds half to even; `^` and `ln`/`exp`/`cos` agree to ~1 ulp across engines. NetLogo integers are 64-bit, so the 32-bit multiply was done in 16-bit limbs (`imul`) to stay byte-identical to the Python PRNG.

### NOT MODELLED

Rental landlord logic (split incentives), energy communities, moving/inheritance, phasing, vendor capacity or price response, collective loans or reserve funds (a single owner who cannot pay blocks a shared measure), the state's budget constraint, behavioural effects beyond the monthly consideration draw, anything social — no ideology, no solidarity lever. Non-refundable rebates with no tax payable give nothing, by construction.

### OUTPUTS

Plots: adoption by quintile (1 = lowest income; rental units count in denominators), cumulative state spend (vendor payments at adoption + rebates when they arrive), cumulative household bills saved, cost per retrofit. Monitors: month, adopted units, public cost, bills saved, cost per retrofit, cost decomposition (base + markup), tenure and building-state counts, and by quintile: adoption, can-front-cost (could still finance what they owe now), rebate per euro, net cash, canvass and leisure hours. One patch per building is coloured by adoption state.

Frame semantics match `v3/docs/frame-schema.md`. Events (one row per retrofit) and votes are counted (`votes-passed`) but not recorded as a log.

### PARAMETERS

Widgets set the run's parameters at **setup**. Household demographics (income log-normal(10.3, 0.5), flat 25% tax above a €10,000 allowance, log-normal savings with 3-month-income mean, 5–25 year stays, the quintile work-hour profile, waking/committed hours, free-time spread, canvassing parameters, energy growth 0/3/8% p.a., scene rules) are code constants, clearly marked PLACEHOLDER in the code; edit the code to change them. `horizon-months` bounds both the simulation length and the consideration rule (households past their stay do not consider; their savings are still counted while they stay).

An experiment named **smoke-test** is embedded (full 180-month run, reports adoptions, state cost and bills saved) so the file can be checked headless with:
`netlogo-headless.sh --model great-retrofit-v3.nlogox --experiment smoke-test --table -`
]]></info>
